From 1760f97f7e428026443b3a3b8b2535c5f67f360e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: n0madic <800462+n0madic@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 16:49:38 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] GS: super-sampled, double-resolution output with the game's
 final blit replaced

PS2_GS_SSAA (default 8; 1, 4, 8 or 16) sets paraLLEl-GS's super-sampling.
paraLLEl-GS scans out one sample per pixel unless it scans out at twice the
resolution, so any rate above 1 also enables high_resolution_scanout. Many
games, Soulcalibur III among them, render into one buffer and blit it into
the displayed one (here a 640x448 PSMCT32 buffer, blended into a 512x512
PSMCT16 buffer with 27% of the previous picture); the samples would not
survive that blit. With super-sampling the renderer therefore samples
super-sampled textures per sample and enables paraLLEl-GS's
backbuffer_promotion hack, which scans out the blit's source instead.
PS2_GS_SSAA=1 keeps the exact display.

The host keeps the GPU renderer's picture at its own size instead of
cropping it to 640x512, and the window texture follows that size.
